result:
Fixed. `std::os::unix::net::SocketAddr::from_abstract_name` takes the abstract name *without* the namespace's leading NUL — it writes the NUL into `sun_path[0]` itself. Tokio was passing the full path bytes, NUL included, so binding/connecting to `"\0aaa"` registered the abstract name `"\0aaa"` (kernel `sun_path` = `\0\0aaa`), and `as_abstract_name()` returned `b"\0aaa"` instead of `b"aaa"`.

Both call sites now strip the leading byte they already tested for:

- `tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:84` (`UnixListener::bind`)
- `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:80` (`UnixStream::connect`)

```rust
if os_str_bytes.starts_with(b"\0") {
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(&os_str_bytes[1..])?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

The slice is safe: it's guarded by the `starts_with(b"\0")` check, so index 1 is always in bounds (an empty remainder yields the valid unnamed-length-0 abstract name). `UnixDatagram` and `UnixSocket` don't have this path — they go through `socket2::SockAddr::unix`/mio and were untouched by the bug.

Verification: `abstract_socket_name` fails on the unmodified tree and passes with the fix; `uds_stream`, `uds_datagram`, `uds_split`, and `uds_cred` all pass (22 tests, 0 failures).
